# Line endings are a property of this REPOSITORY, not of each developer's git config.
#
# Until this file existed, every line ending here was decided by whatever `core.autocrlf` the
# cloning machine happened to have. On this machine it is `true`, so the index is all-LF and the
# working tree is all-CRLF -- and the consequence is that a script which writes a file WITHOUT
# doing that conversion produces a working tree with mixed line endings that `git status` reports
# as clean, because git normalises CRLF back to LF on the way in and the script's LF lines are
# already LF. Two files were found in exactly that state (`gradle.properties`, `JobCard.kt`):
# invisible to git, invisible to the build, and the same mechanism that once made a revert regex
# assume `\n` against a CRLF file and left a test looking verified when it was not.
#
# `text=auto` pins the normalisation that was previously incidental. It is a no-op against the
# current object store, which is already all-LF -- `git add --renormalize .` changes nothing.
* text=auto

# gradlew is executed by /bin/sh on Unix and CI. A CR at the end of its shebang or of any
# `exec` line is a syntax error there, so this one cannot be left to a local setting.
gradlew text eol=lf

# cmd.exe is the other direction: it parses labels and `goto` by byte offset and mis-handles
# LF-only batch files. gradlew.bat is currently stored LF, which is the fragile form.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Declared explicitly rather than left to git's content heuristic, which is right about these
# but has no reason to be trusted with the app's only launcher assets.
*.jar binary
*.webp binary
*.png binary
*.apk binary
*.aab binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks binary
